#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120884} :activity/id #uuid "dcdc8ecc-c5d8-56ba-b664-0870beb832b2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121126 :tutorial/content "A **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^n$ that is closed  under vector addition and scalar multiplication. In other words: \n\n$H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nFor example, consider the line $L$ with equation $y=3x$ in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$ \n\nFirst, notice that we can express the points on $L$ as the following set:\n\n![](images/1f/1f380f9928da2dd44ff157a72fce97c02ae44626df515d52ece6ff5feee302d2.svg)\n\nIn other words, $L$ contains all vectors of the form ![](images/90/90996c2ffae29c3bd9cd3ab398cca4dd7e1b8fbe275de8520a72a16e504fb0f3.svg) where $x$ is a real number.\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb R^2,$ we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/59/59be22e80d0be32c84a8c2bdb2f2c96b23a0941d4aba26bdefa078fd299c137c.svg) and ![](images/d7/d78ea4fcfbd469e88b7fe46e28ac6f396a538c577f4defdd1b3d60e60df3c4b7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}+3{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} \\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/75/759c7883ad2772f20a090ad2f8c5dfe01031ca31e2b5f0e7681c945c9256c91d.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ 3{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ 3\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $y=3x.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n**Note:** Since the second condition must be satisfied for any $c,$ including $c=0,$ we can conclude that every subspace must contain the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}.$ So, if a set does not contain $\\mathbf{0}$ then it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor instance, the set \n\n![](images/a1/a15e0b4fc4544421e572d15b8fd016c3fa8721e0ecf3f8808a469c3252d1f6d6.svg)\n\ndoes *not* contain the zero vector (since $0 \\neq 0 + 1$), and consequently it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "ee2c580c-5386-5e06-9b58-e9e1dd398cd3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6853 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7f009ee8-4a5e-5da7-b539-b2991cbd9d04" :step/math-academy-id 41645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119926 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119500 :question/id #uuid "d2cb7f5f-ad04-5e04-b2da-0f9b555442a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10974"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19c33c51-bf11-5a81-a0fd-8381d8e0b9d7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048499} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275389} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "a7eb434d-3210-5f2f-b5ec-18781d0fa02d" :step/math-academy-id 41646 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119927 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119501 :question/id #uuid "d0d76105-c49e-5cf8-af57-bd74989e895b" :question/math-academy-id "e-10977"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83796fe8-f045-5dbc-94d1-6d6e031f3ed8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425728}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Line Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "911d42c3-5fd0-515e-abcd-cfaab6aa6926" :step/math-academy-id 41660 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119502 :question/id #uuid "d2199de1-4bfe-5589-95dd-f9bb7c339437" :question/math-academy-id "e-10980"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f3018fd-9d16-578f-b050-966890844c9b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376068}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Plane Is a Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "8b4b4898-6c15-52d9-96fe-d57251b08369" :step/math-academy-id 41667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121127 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n*Closure under vector addition:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n*Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n $\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nLet $L$ be the line in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,1,-2).$ Is $L$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ \n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n![](images/25/25fa34994b33e0d11fd8990dddbfda276857e699018b4701c6e2eaf13acb0c20.svg)\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in\\mathbb R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $L$ is a subspace, we need to check the two conditions above:\n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $L,$ namely, ![](images/d7/d7f1a3cf1625ff12bc96324dc428e1c95d271d8a72de603b671f722b9173281d.svg) and ![](images/25/255fc61225b017a51ae60b1d157787ac4b60fe596ebaa8296b48053b87b2eae7.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{1} \\\\ -2{v}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1}+4{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2{u}_{1})+(-2{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1}) \\\\ {u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $L,$ namely, ![](images/7f/7f7df4272eae1975b5802d54dfae499c528661ba0ae82faff45bb26f6cfab276.svg) Then, for any scalar $c,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{1} \\\\ -2{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{1} \\\\ (-2)\\cdot c{u}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $\\dfrac x4=y=\\dfrac z{-2}.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in L. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $L$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0bb214-7f56-557a-82f7-baeef815457e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6859 :tutorial/title "Lines as Subspaces of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "124c571f-28f0-56fa-a68d-76ef241f4618" :step/math-academy-id 41674 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121128 :tutorial/content "Again, recall that $H$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nConsider the plane $\\Pi$ with equation $z=4x-2y$ in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ \n\nNotice that $\\Pi$ contains vectors of the form \n\n![](images/95/956757aa0ce5b4721db582cd9d03dffaa21b1fdf6f8c60b5c6465f9cf012201d.svg)\n\nIs $\\Pi$ a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$ To find out, we need to check the two conditions above. \n\n- Let's pick two arbitrary vectors in $\\Pi,$ namely, ![](images/da/da46ac38012b220c5cf956076a81b76a21eb1242d21fae69f071531401d5d41d.svg) and ![](images/3e/3ee33188044335cd84f42b7621a5d255fdab972d89bef3d816c2b5a4da93dab0.svg) If we compute their sum, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u+v & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}+4{v}_{1}-2{v}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}+{v}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2}+{v}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot ({u}_{1}+{v}_{1})-2\\cdot ({u}_{2}+{v}_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- Let's pick an arbitrary vector in $\\Pi,$ say, ![](images/27/277ea18a8e1721fdaa880025e3abb4fcb1ff3e8ec96730ca7597fbd584ef6500.svg) Then, for any scalar $c$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c\\cdot u & =c\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ 4{u}_{1}-2{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}c{u}_{1} \\\\ c{u}_{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot c{u}_{1}-2\\cdot c{u}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $z=4x-2y.$ Therefore, $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}\\in \\Pi. \\quad {\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nThe set $\\Pi$ satisfies both conditions. So, we can conclude that it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3fcbfc6c-e5f6-5344-8ddd-779b625a03ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6860 :tutorial/title "Planes as Subspace of Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "2d8c8e20-696b-5df3-beaf-a78962ce0420" :step/math-academy-id 41675 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120887}}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120047}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417223} :activity/id #uuid "9b35dfb4-fca3-545e-9624-8422a0c70703" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046 :topic/id #uuid "7cf616eb-2b54-52d2-8407-1b2fc912b2d9" :topic/math-academy-id 1853} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417220 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32e12cd0-8ad2-5342-a83f-ccbf2317d4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272841} {:answer/id #uuid "5d33accc-abb6-5ca8-91ac-3f16818dfc7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273187} {:answer/id #uuid "a5481f96-dc39-5a01-87f3-c8a786036d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273816} {:answer/id #uuid "aed41c16-0b33-5b30-868f-7fa0cdda1855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273898} {:answer/id #uuid "b1977fbb-4f99-54cc-a369-bf10fbbab7ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273816} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef527d9b-d04c-5f20-b418-bb16551ec425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "84592f87-8902-5e5c-972a-3aba1b1c18c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-157377" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(1,1,1)$ and $(2,-1,2).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx-1=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=z-1.\n$$\n\nBut this line does not pass through the origin since \n\n$$\n(0)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (0)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider the vectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ from $L$ (the position vectors of the given points). Then,\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHowever, the point $(3,0,3)$ does not belong to $L$ since\n\n$$\n(3)-1\\ne \\frac{(0)-1}{-2}\\ne (3)-1.\\,\\times \n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "1436249c-5de8-51a2-9062-17b0c4a22818" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417224}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417221 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21412424-3f98-56c9-a40e-7ec7f1e6afe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272682} {:answer/id #uuid "72dbcfa4-b46d-516a-9d19-dbb2c60456ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273371} {:answer/id #uuid "975e374e-a0f4-567e-a122-9789d1e8aee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273695} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e0e2ea-d6d0-51a4-81b2-ad94bc45782c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "All the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273989} {:answer/id #uuid "c035c762-64f6-55f3-92af-3f6fc55eb23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274049}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273989} :answer-field/id #uuid "d137125c-728c-5b38-a1dd-d174752da874" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f036c2d4-285c-5c5e-a300-6732346f17c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-157374" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line in ${R}^{3}$ that passes through the points $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,-2,3).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nFirst, note that the direction vector of the line $L$ is \n\n$$\nd=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in R,$ and the corresponding Cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, from the definition of $L$ we have that the point $(0,0,0)$ is in $L.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ -2b \\\\ 3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ (-2a)+(-2b) \\\\ 3a+3b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ -2(a+b) \\\\ 3(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -2a \\\\ 3a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(-2a) \\\\ k(3a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ -2(ka) \\\\ 3(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the conditions $x=-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"All the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "1821a5bc-a723-5459-b893-12f5b2f84d53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417220}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417222 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56367304-94a3-532f-932a-a6a009eb1f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273119} {:answer/id #uuid "8598a628-17cc-5fd1-9b55-e3ad503f0213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273532} {:answer/id #uuid "8c543d58-af14-5083-bca6-698e1e93ba2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273597} {:answer/id #uuid "92fb9d1c-aa8d-5c76-b479-e9d488ea1f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273665} {:answer/id #uuid "c6de225f-fcf6-5420-b1a7-34d4eefa9309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186273665} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b3ab815-cda1-53c4-82ce-396893f98644" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "4f59c175-a7f1-58ec-b16f-fd24b9c9dd63" :question/math-academy-id "q-156685" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-y-z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n- $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n- $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $Π$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ x-y-1\\end{aligned}],\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (0) & =(0)-(0)-(1) \\\\ 0 & =-1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ -1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x-y-1 \\\\ (-1) & =(1)-(0)-1 \\\\ -1 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "647ef6bb-9fcf-5b00-95cb-b3da5691fc0c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417221}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417223 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44a4e1dc-a2d7-5163-8db3-bd5e766783de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417213} {:answer/id #uuid "5175130c-a0e7-5993-877f-e784135b6082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417214} {:answer/id #uuid "7d69583b-f76a-5611-8cb8-4022da72fc78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417215} {:answer/id #uuid "99e8273a-2cc4-5606-ade8-2988cc10652d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417216} {:answer/id #uuid "a149c6f5-ef3c-51ad-997e-db08bfab777c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417217}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417214} :answer-field/id #uuid "7336d919-b96c-5024-ae89-e9caaf4e41d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "feee3179-e7d1-55ab-bcac-f334cca10495" :question/math-academy-id "q-156620" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the line with equation $4x=2y$ in ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $L$ contains the zero-vector\n- $L$ is closed under vector addition\n- $L$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n- $L$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of the zero-vector satisfy the equation $4x=2y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =2y \\\\ 4(0) & =2(0) \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]\\in L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2a+2b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}a+b \\\\ 2(a+b)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $a+b\\in L.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]\\in L$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k\\cdot a & =k\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ k(2a)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}ka \\\\ 2(ka)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich satisfies the condition $4x=2y.$ Therefore, $k\\cdot a\\in L.$\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are true, $L$ must be a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "a6b1b4f4-42fd-518f-9611-64b9f9fd951d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417222}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417224 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c083987-1ed3-5e4c-bd55-30281759ca50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272628} {:answer/id #uuid "497c74c6-a649-5c4c-bd1f-87610ac4d409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273013} {:answer/id #uuid "8582d41d-a676-5252-951a-4cbdfbe11cbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273531} {:answer/id #uuid "d2fcea74-601e-54dd-9002-31e86a554eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274234} {:answer/id #uuid "d613a78a-999c-595e-80c8-e5ccdf36c46f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186274259} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d26109-e4a8-5040-b8ff-5e1ca19040b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "99eda82b-48bc-5827-9c44-f2725d46a5d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-157476" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $x-2y+2z=3$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $Π$ contains the zero-vector\n\nII. $Π$ is closed under vector addition\n\nIII. $Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIV. $Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+2y-2z \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,y,z\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (0)-2(0)+2(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let's consider $a=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\in Π$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3+1 \\\\ 0+0 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y+2z & =3 \\\\ (4)-2(0)+2(1) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "f20bfc1c-6733-50b5-9d02-7dbeed933bee"}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417212}]}
